The "mirrors legacy behavior" claim IS supported by the codebase. See `src/domain/space/about/SpaceAboutPage.tsx:17-20`:

```ts
const goBackPage = permissions.canRead ? space.about.profile.url : undefined;
const goBackSteps = permissions.canRead ? undefined : 2;
const backToParentPage = useBackWithDefaultUrl(goBackPage, goBackSteps);
```

When `!canRead`, the legacy MUI page passes `goBackSteps: 2` to `useBackWithDefaultUrl`, which steps browser history back two entries. Our CRD implementation uses the exact same hook with the same arguments (line 38-41 of CrdSpaceAboutPage.tsx). The rationale is as described: it exits both the inaccessible Space home AND the About URL itself.

`MetadataToken` is typed as `int` with a default of `0`. Metadata token `0` (the nil/null token, `0x00000000`) is technically not a valid type token, so using 0-as-sentinel could work in practice — but the spec says the field is populated "when available", implying it may be absent. That intent is much clearer as `int?`:

```csharp
// before
public int MetadataToken { get; init; }

// after — explicit about absence
public int? MetadataToken { get; init; }
```

With `int?`, a `null` unambiguously means "not available" and the "at least one of" contract at line 80 becomes straightforward to validate. Using `0` as a sentinel also risks confusion with any future change that legitimately handles module-level or assembly-level tokens (which start at 0x20000001 etc.).

**Prior finding still open** (flagged in the first review pass): the heading "Handlers that only care about success guard at the top" does not match the example below it — the example shows a *dual-condition* guard (`result is not Success && update.SolutionUpdate is not <subtype>`), which is the motivating-scenario handler, not a success-only one. A success-only handler would simply be:

```csharp
if (result is not HotReloadOperationResult.Success)
    return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
```

Either fix the heading to "Handlers that only act on Success or a specific SolutionUpdate subtype guard at the top" or split into two examples (trivial success-only first, then the motivating-scenario form).

The code sketch shows `FailedTypes` and `UpdatedTypes` as additions to `HotReloadClientOperationEvent`, but the truncation section (lines 101–106) introduces two further fields (`OmittedFailedTypeCount` / `OmittedUpdatedTypeCount`) that also belong in this sketch. An implementer reading only the schema block would miss those fields. Suggest extending it:

```suggestion
/// <summary>Identities of the types whose UI update failed to apply (per-element isolation).</summary>
public ImmutableArray<HotReloadTypeOutcome> FailedTypes { get; init; } = ImmutableArray<HotReloadTypeOutcome>.Empty;

/// <summary>Identities of the types whose UI update applied successfully.</summary>
public ImmutableArray<HotReloadTypeOutcome> UpdatedTypes { get; init; } = ImmutableArray<HotReloadTypeOutcome>.Empty;

/// <summary>Number of failed-type identities omitted due to the payload cap; 0 when none truncated.</summary>
public int OmittedFailedTypeCount { get; init; }

/// <summary>Number of updated-type identities omitted due to the payload cap; 0 when none truncated.</summary>
public int OmittedUpdatedTypeCount { get; init; }
```
